@* aria-invalid is what turns the control destructive — the border and the ring read it, and a
   screen reader reads the same attribute, so there is one source of truth.

   The description stays muted. It is a description, not an error: it says what the field wants,
   and it says the same thing whether or not the value is wrong. Only FieldError is destructive,
   and this example has none — which is upstream's, and the difference between a form that
   points at the problem and one that turns red all over. *@
<Field Invalid Class="w-[420px]">
    <FieldLabel For="ta-invalid">Message</FieldLabel>
    <Textarea id="ta-invalid" name="message" rows="3" placeholder="Type your message here."
              aria-invalid="true" aria-describedby="ta-invalid-help" />
    <FieldDescription id="ta-invalid-help">Please enter a valid message.</FieldDescription>
</Field>
